WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.410
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.535
MARTIN OMANDER: Hey, Ahmet.

00:00:02.535 --> 00:00:03.743
AHMET ALP BALKAN: Hi, Martin.

00:00:03.743 --> 00:00:04.707
What's going on?

00:00:04.707 --> 00:00:06.540
MARTIN OMANDER: I built
a Cloud Run service,

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:09.450
and I noticed it's slower
for users in Australia

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.190
than for users in the US.

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:12.315
Could you help me fix that?

00:00:12.315 --> 00:00:13.690
AHMET ALP BALKAN:
Sounds like you

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:16.379
might need to put your Cloud Run
services closer to your users.

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:17.740
I can show you how.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:21.170
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:27.728 --> 00:00:28.520
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:32.090
My web application has a
sign-up form with a city field.

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:34.220
As the user types
into the city field,

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:36.050
they will get
autocomplete suggestions

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:37.550
from a Cloud Run service.

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:39.370
AHMET ALP BALKAN: That
sounds good, Martin.

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:41.120
MARTIN OMANDER: And I
deployed the service

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:42.980
to the us-central1 region.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:45.650
When I access it from my
house here in California,

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:48.810
I get response times between
70 and 80 milliseconds.

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:49.935
I'm pretty happy with that.

00:00:49.935 --> 00:00:52.060
AHMET ALP BALKAN: But you're
saying this service is

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:53.925
slower for users in Australia?

00:00:53.925 --> 00:00:54.800
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:56.330
This is what happens
when I access

00:00:56.330 --> 00:01:00.470
the service from a virtual
machine I set up in Sydney.

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:01.220
There.

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:03.800
You see here, the
responses are much slower--

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:06.200
just over 200 milliseconds.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:07.795
Why is that happening, Ahmet?

00:01:07.795 --> 00:01:09.920
AHMET ALP BALKAN: Right
now, your Cloud Run service

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:13.460
is running in us-central1, which
is in the Iowa data center.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:16.310
People in Australia are so
much further away from Iowa

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:20.060
than you are, so naturally
their requests will take longer.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.370
For many applications,
this is not a big deal.

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:24.890
If the response times you
just showed me are acceptable,

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:27.980
you can keep serving all
your users from one location.

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:29.990
But for some
interactive services,

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:33.620
like your autocomplete service,
every millisecond counts.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:36.980
So your users in Australia
would see a better performance

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:39.890
if you could take this
autocomplete service closer

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:41.570
to them.

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:43.660
This means you need to
deploy the same Cloud Run

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:46.498
service to a Google data
center in Australia.

00:01:46.498 --> 00:01:47.290
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:48.830
That sounds great.

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:51.010
But how do I configure
my Cloud Run service

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:53.350
so I can route them to the
nearest Google data center?

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:54.850
AHMET ALP BALKAN:
You don't have to.

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:57.190
The Google Cloud
HTTPS Load Balancer

00:01:57.190 --> 00:02:00.670
lets you use global anycast
IPs, meaning wherever

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:03.280
the user connects from,
they're routed to the nearest

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:07.090
Google data center, and then the
Cloud Load Balancer will route

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:09.310
the request to the
nearest Cloud Run service

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:11.218
that you have deployed.

00:02:11.218 --> 00:02:12.010
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:13.090
Let's do it.

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:16.510
But I'm an application
developer, not a network admin.

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:18.113
Are there any
instructions for this?

00:02:18.113 --> 00:02:19.030
AHMET ALP BALKAN: Yes.

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:20.770
I just sent you the
link to a web page

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:22.150
that describes the process.

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:24.400
Basically, you have to
set up an IP address,

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:26.800
a TLS certificate, then
tie it all together

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:28.420
by creating a load balancer.

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:29.530
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:30.970
Let's see-- pulling it up here.

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:34.910
It looks like it's just a
list of gcloud commands.

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:38.110
Looks like I can enter
the names of my services,

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:41.728
copy the commands, and then I
just run these in my terminal?

00:02:41.728 --> 00:02:43.270
AHMET ALP BALKAN:
Yeah, that's right.

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:46.240
Also, you need to update the
DNS records for your domain name

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:49.000
from your registrar to send
the traffic to the IP address

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:50.000
that you just created.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:51.400
It's all in the instructions.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:52.275
MARTIN OMANDER: Cool.

00:02:52.275 --> 00:02:55.330
I will run these gcloud commands
and update the DNS record.

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:58.060
It can take a while
for DNS to propagate.

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:00.070
See you back here in 30 minutes?

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:01.827
AHMET ALP BALKAN: Sounds good.

00:03:01.827 --> 00:03:02.910
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:03.900
We're back.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:04.920
What now?

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:05.670
AHMET ALP BALKAN:
Let's first make

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:07.837
sure that you haven't broken
anything for your users

00:03:07.837 --> 00:03:08.490
in the US.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:09.750
Do you want to make a request?

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:10.950
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah, yes.

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:11.870
Let's see.

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:15.690
Trying it out now from
here in California.

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:16.190
Ah.

00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:16.940
There.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:17.740
OK, good.

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:19.407
It's still working,
and it's still fast.

00:03:19.407 --> 00:03:20.573
AHMET ALP BALKAN: Excellent.

00:03:20.573 --> 00:03:22.780
Now, let's check if the
response time has improved

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:25.410
for your users in Australia.

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:26.280
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:29.040
Pulling up the web page now.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:32.450
Entering the first
letter of a city name.

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:35.010
Yeah, that's a lot faster.

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:37.420
Before, it was over
200 milliseconds.

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:40.470
Now, most of these
requests took less than 20.

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:42.510
And it shows here that
it's serving from a data

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:44.250
center in Australia.

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:48.090
But why is Sydney faster
than what I get from my house

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:49.017
here in California?

00:03:49.017 --> 00:03:50.350
AHMET ALP BALKAN: Good question.

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:51.933
This is because the
nearest deployment

00:03:51.933 --> 00:03:54.240
that you have right
now is in us-central1,

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.250
which is in Iowa data center.

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:58.110
You could deploy
this to us-west2,

00:03:58.110 --> 00:03:59.910
which is in the Los
Angeles data center,

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:02.830
to get even better response
times from your home.

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:04.980
In fact, there are no
downsides to deploying

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:07.320
all available regions,
because you pay a fixed

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:08.820
price for the load balancer.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:11.520
And since Cloud Run charges
you based on the requests,

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:13.770
there is no downside to
deploying your application

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:14.270
everywhere.

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:15.353
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:04:15.353 --> 00:04:17.160
So my current deployment
is working well

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:20.970
for users in the US and
in Australia right now.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:23.550
But what happens if a user
is in another place-- let's

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:24.630
say Singapore?

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:26.547
I haven't deployed my
Cloud Run service there.

00:04:26.547 --> 00:04:28.213
AHMET ALP BALKAN:
Google's load balancer

00:04:28.213 --> 00:04:30.810
is smart enough to route them
to the closest Cloud Run service

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:33.390
that you have deployed
behind the load balancer.

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:34.558
Why don't you try it?

00:04:34.558 --> 00:04:35.350
MARTIN OMANDER: OK.

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:38.640
I set up a virtual
machine here in Singapore.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:39.240
Let's see.

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:41.800
Opening the web page.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:44.730
Oh, looks like they get just
over 100 milliseconds' response

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:45.880
time.

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:48.570
And here-- we can see the data
was served from the Cloud Run

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:50.162
service in Sydney.

00:04:50.162 --> 00:04:51.120
AHMET ALP BALKAN: Nice.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:53.580
And if you don't want to
run a lot of gcloud commands

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:55.800
to deploy this
application manually,

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:58.290
you can write a Terraform
module and easily

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:00.270
deploy your application
to all regions.

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:02.730
We'll put a link in the
description for that.

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:03.930
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, cool.

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:06.385
But, Ahmet, what about
my users in Antarctica?

00:05:06.385 --> 00:05:08.010
AHMET ALP BALKAN:
Ah, serious question.

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:10.020
Do you mean the researchers
and the penguins?

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:11.956
Maybe we'll get to it someday.

00:05:11.956 --> 00:05:14.410
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah,
yes, the penguins.

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:16.960
Everyone, thank you
so much for watching.

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:19.050
Please leave the
comments below and let

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:21.360
us know if there are
any other topics you'd

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:22.410
like us to discuss.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:25.760
[MUSIC PLAYING]

